<meta type=article title="Dataset consumers — one <data> store, three readers (#313)">
</meta>

<section | One store, three readers>

The `<data>` block at the back of this document declares three `<dataset>`
stores — a CSV table, a Mermaid diagram source, and a Python snippet — as
opaque bytes under shared ids. Each is consumed below by a different
element through the same neutral `@id` hand-off: storage is opaque, and
interpretation belongs to the consuming element (`notes/specs/data-store.md`).

<sub-section | A table from a CSV dataset>

`<table src="@sales">` pulls the stored `<dataset #sales csv | …>` and
renders it as a grid. The table names no format word, so the dataset's
`csv` hint applies.

<table #tab:sales src="@sales" />

<sub-section | A diagram from a Mermaid dataset>

`<diagram mermaid src="@flow">` feeds the stored Mermaid source verbatim
into the engine wrapper. The dataset's `mermaid` format hint matches the
named engine, so it is accepted; a hint that disagreed (a `csv` dataset,
say) would be a visible asset-error instead.

<diagram #fig:flow mermaid src="@flow" />

<sub-section | A code listing from a dataset>

`<code src="@snippet">` renders the stored bytes as a verbatim code body.
The dataset's `python` hint seeds the `language-python` highlight class; the
bytes render verbatim (a `#` or `*` in the source is never markdown-parsed).

<code src="@snippet" />

<data>
<dataset #sales csv | quarter,revenue
Q1,100
Q2,120
Q3,145>
<dataset #flow mermaid>
graph LR
  A[Author] --> B[eHTML]
  B --> C[HTML + CSS + JS]
</dataset>
<dataset #snippet python | # compute the circle area
import math
radius = 2.0
area = math.pi * radius ** 2>
</data>
